+# Task 0008.7.2.2 — Cross-Scene Projection & Empty-Node Crash Hotfix
+
+## Purpose
+
+Correct three regressions discovered after `0008.7.2.1 Sprite Tree Ownership Hotfix`:
+
+1. Scratch Sprite Adapter nodes created in one scene were not reliably projected while another scene was active.
+2. Adapter nodes with no Runtime or compatibility children still rendered a disclosure arrow.
+3. Activating an empty adapter node could crash Project Explorer with `ReferenceError: visitVisibleNode is not defined`.
+
+## Ownership rule
+
+Every original Scratch Sprite continues to have one canonical Project Explorer row: its Runtime Scratch Sprite Adapter node.
+
+Active-scene bindings are shown as `Scratch Sprite · Bound`. Bindings belonging to inactive scenes remain in the project Runtime Graph and are shown as `Scratch Sprite · offline`. The temporary Scratch `target.id` is not retained for inactive bindings.
+
+## Corrections
+
+- Project-wide binding indexes are rebuilt from every Scene binding sidecar, not only from scenes reconciled during the current service lifetime.
+- Missing inactive Runtime adapter nodes are recreated from persistent binding records.
+- A Scene Runtime transition guard defers VM target events between `before-load`/`before-reload` and `loaded`/`reloaded`.
+- New Target lists can no longer be reconciled against the previous `activeSceneId` during snapshot deserialization.
+- Project Explorer determines child existence from `React.Children.toArray(children)`, so null conditional children do not create false disclosure arrows.
+- The legacy compatibility-tree traversal function now has a stable lexical scope and is available to Runtime adapter traversal.
+- Scene System version is `0.7.2.2`.
+
+## Compatibility
+
+No binding schema or Runtime Node persistent DTO version was changed. Existing `scratchSpriteBindings` sidecars are hydrated as-is. Inactive bindings are projected as Offline and become Bound after their scene loads.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+This hotfix does not address asset download failures from `assets.scratch.mit.edu`, deprecated React lifecycle warnings in `SpriteSelectorItem`, or Chrome Built-In AI notices. Those log entries are unrelated to the Project Explorer crash fixed here.
